## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a pragmatic, scalable program that links people, process, and outcomes. Evaluates how to allocate limited time for long‑term health without killing velocity, and how to guard against gaming.

## Key Concepts

- Team health budgeting and non-delivery work
- Cross‑team governance in multi-time‑zone setups
- ROI metrics: defect density, cycle time, retention
- Anti-gaming controls and sunset reviews

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple scoring for health proposals
function scoreProposal(p) {
  const impact = p.impact * 0.4;
  const risk = (1 - p.timeliness) * 0.3;
  const effort = 1 / (p.effort + 0.01);
  return impact + risk + effort;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle proposals that seem high ROI but strain critical squads?
- What metrics would you track to ensure long-term health gains persist beyond one quarter?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to triage, harden reliability under pressure, and coordinate cross-team remediation with business impact.

## Key Concepts

- Incident containment and rollback
- Safe fallbacks, circuit breakers, backoff strategies
- Cross-team governance and ownership
- Metrics: latency, payout SLA, MTTR, post-incident learning

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: simple circuit breaker
class Breaker {
  constructor(threshold) {
    this.failures = 0;
    this.threshold = threshold;
    this.open = false;
  }
  call(fn) {
    if (this.open) throw new Error('Breaker is open');
    return fn().catch(() => {
      this.failures++;
      if (this.failures > this.threshold) this.open = true;
      throw;
    });
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure success after the fix? 
- What would you include in the post-incident report for audit and compliance?

## Why This Is Asked

